{
  "dom_id": "step-q103887",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14190611/assets/00a21418964ec8f7802eb8df1a5ec7afd12f3bc3fe5303a0aed34b1936b1827a.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on the interval $[-2,4].$ The graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $5.5$, $2.5$ and $4$ respectively (from left to right). Find $f(2)$ if $f(-2)=-4.$",
  "worked_solution": "From the fundamental theorem of calculus, we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =f(2)-f(-2) \\\\ {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =f(2)-(-4) \\\\ f(2) & =-4+{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, remember that the integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where we take the area as negative if the curve lies below the $x$-axis. So, using the labels on the graph, the integral is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x=5.5+(-2.5)=3.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the value of the integral, we can find $f(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =-4+{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4+3 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
